WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.980
Hey, this is Presh Talwalkar.

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:05.600
What is the value of this mathematical expression?

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:08.980
This math problem has gone viral

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:11.960
and it has received millions of comments on

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:16.760
Facebook, Twitter, Youtube, and other social media sites.

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:21.620
In this video, I'm going to present the correct answer.

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:27.480
The problem is an example of the order of operations.

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:31.420
These are commonly refered to as PEMDAS or BODMAS.

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:35.380
This refers to evaluating the parentheses/brackets,

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:37.460
then the exponents/orders,

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:39.520
Then multiplication and division

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:41.940
And finally, addition-subtraction.

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:44.920
You have two operations of the same precedence,

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:47.220
you want to evaluate them from left to right.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:51.600
The first of the problem has no controversy.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:58.000
This expression contains a parenthetical expression which must be evaluated first.

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:01.200
1+2 is inside the parentheses,

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:04.120
so we'll evaluate 1+2 to get 3.

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:08.260
Now, the question is what to do next.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:15.140
If you this into google, wolfram alpha, or pretty much any scientific calculator,

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:17.940
the thing that's going to happen next is

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:23.520
all of these will interpret the parentheses as an implicit multiplication.

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:30.720
So this two parenthesis three will be converted into 2 times 3.

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:35.200
Now we continue the order of operations.

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:39.480
This expression only contains multiplication and division,

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:44.940
These are operators of equal precedence, so we'll evaluate them from left to right.

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:49.620
Starting on the left, we have 6 divided by 2

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:52.760
6 divided by 2 is equal to 3

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:58.440
We then have 3 multiplied by 3 - one final multiplication.

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:02.340
And that gets us to the correct answer of 9.

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:05.880
This is, without a doubt, the correct answer to

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:08.610
this expression as written according to

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:11.220
the modern usage of the order of operations.

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:14.540
So why did this problem cause so much controversy?

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:18.060
Well, there is another answer

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:20.180
that you could argue from a historical

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:23.610
perspective, so I actually found some

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:25.800
documentation that the order of

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:28.800
operations did have a slightly different

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:33.560
understanding in certain texts in 1917 or before.

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:38.340
So the first part of the

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:40.800
equation is the same as before: we have a

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:43.170
parenthetical expression and this should

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:45.510
be evaluated first we have 1 plus 2 and

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:49.580
that becomes 3. The debate then centers

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:52.560
around this division symbol.

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:55.860
So what does it mean that we have 6 divided by 2

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:58.680
parentheses 3? While there were textbooks

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:01.230
and there was a lot of usage that if you

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:04.100
had this division symbol where you had

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:06.880
something on the left divided by something on the right,

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:08.960
this was understood to mean

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:11.760
you want to divide the entire product on the left

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:14.780
by the entire product on the right.

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:17.780
So, for example, if a textbook wrote

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:20.820
"x divided by 2y" with this division symbol

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:24.450
they actually did mean x divided by

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:27.080
parentheses 2y. You wanted to take

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:31.180
2y as the entire product and have that as your denominator.

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:37.700
So under this historical usage - which is a special

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:41.300
exception to the order of operations (and we don't use it anymore) -

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:46.000
you would want to take this product on the right

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:51.870
as your divisor. So applying this rule would

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:57.940
then lead to the expression 6 over 2 x 3.

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:01.640
We will now convert the multiplication

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:04.500
in the denominator so that 2 times 3 is

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:08.370
equal to 6 and we now have one division

00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:12.480
which is 6/6 and that's equal to 1.

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:15.660
So, many people argue that one is a correct

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:17.790
answer, and there is some historical

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:19.919
justification of this because of the way

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:22.180
that texts used to use the division symbol.

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:25.000
I would suggest this is probably

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:27.680
because of some historical artifact about typesetting:

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:28.900
it would have been much easier

00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:32.160
to write the division symbol and have

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:33.509
the understanding you want to divide

00:04:33.509 --> 00:04:35.100
everything on the left by everything on

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:37.380
the right; you wouldn't need to have an

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:39.360
expression where you write a numerator

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:41.639
over a denominator that would take a lot

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:43.740
more vertical space and you also would

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:45.820
need to keep putting parentheses everywhere.

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:48.320
This would be just something that would be understood.

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:53.260
Today we don't use this practice, because it can be confusing.

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:56.180
Instead, we follow the order of operations.

00:04:56.180 --> 00:04:57.860
If we want to have a fraction,

00:04:57.860 --> 00:05:00.720
we will put it as an expression like 6 over 6

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:02.760
which is written here.

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:05.960
So the correct answer to this problem is 9, but there is

00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:11.620
some historical justification for the answer 1, but it's not how we'd interpret the problem today.

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:15.380
Did you get to the correct answer of 9?

